{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-47779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1, & x<0.5 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2}, & x\\ge 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[1,2]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(0,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(x-1)=(0.5)-1=-0.5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(4-{x}^{2})=4-(0.5)^{2}=3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0.5$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.5.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[1,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0.5,$ which is not in the interval $[1,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abe9b63882a7bd3d2cbce6e4abf7db12e32ae4f1e15077693a77b1b7da76f49"} {"question" "q-47779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\n- $(0,5]$\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[0,5]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abe9b63882a7bd3d2cbce6e4abf7db12e32ae4f1e15077693a77b1b7da76f49"} {"question" "q-47787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-47787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}?$   \n\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[1,10]$\n- $(-1,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,0]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=-1.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,10]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval  $(-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abe9b63882a7bd3d2cbce6e4abf7db12e32ae4f1e15077693a77b1b7da76f49"} {"question" "q-73482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1), & x>1 \\\\ x-1, & x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[-1,2]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abe9b63882a7bd3d2cbce6e4abf7db12e32ae4f1e15077693a77b1b7da76f49"} {"question" "q-73499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-73499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-131068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[2,4]$\n- $[0,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-131068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2x)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[2,4].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[0,1)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-131068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abe9b63882a7bd3d2cbce6e4abf7db12e32ae4f1e15077693a77b1b7da76f49"} {"question" "q-131068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-131068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"} {"question" "q-131068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038091/state.json" "file_sha256" "7658b8e0eae39a74d7eceb453f483e8154e525b7b5e769da2dd073e103849aa1"}] "usage" {"q-47779" [] "q-47787" []} "basis" 1288 "retractions" [[17592186176411 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n$f(x) = {x - 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0.5 \\\\ 4 - x^{2},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0.5$.\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n1. $(0, 1)$\n2. $[0, 2]$\n3. $[1, 2]$"] [17592186176414 :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y = 